                  "hidden": ""
                },
                "ImageDescription": {
                  "value": "Dronninghaugen in Gaukslandsneset/Gaukslandstoe, Vinje. Local legend says it is the grave of Jorunn Joklekåpa. Just on the edge of the field there are two flat graves (flatmarksgrav), with some bones found there. One of them is dated 255-425 CE. Behind Dronninghaugen there are 2 or 3 burial mounds. Another mound with a menhir is in the forest just behind the road, at the right corner of the photo. The legend tells about war between king of Øvrebø hillfort on the opposite side of the lake, and king Gause of Gauksland, and Jorunn was the queen of Gauksland, according to Rikard Berge. Arne Vinje, the former gouvernor, tells, that the battle was on the water, so Gause drowned, and that is why he got no mound called after him. Jorunn Joklekåpa is know from a ballad sungen in Telemark, Spind and Agder, where she is an ally of king of Gautland in his war against king Herredag, who’s ally are Thor (the Old Norse god) and Sigurd Svein (Sigurd Fåvnesbane). In another ballad, about count Gunseli, Jorunn Joklekåpa is mentioned too. In the light of that Gunseli is obviously Jormunrek (Ermanaric), she must be Gudrun. According to Molke Moe and Sophus Bugge (see \"Torsvisen i sin norske form\"), the name Joklekåpa is a mix between names of Gjuki and Budli, Brunhild's father in the Völsunga saga.",
                  "source": "commons-desc-page"
                },
                "DateTimeOriginal": {
                  "value": "2026-07-15 16:03:35",
