                  "value": "Dronninghaugen in Gaukslandsneset/Gaukslandstoe, Vinje. Local legend says it is the grave of Jorunn Joklekåpa. Behind the photographer there are 2 or 3 burial mounds, and two in front behind Dronninghaugen, and two flat graves behind Dronninghaugen dated to the Migration Period. The legend tells about war between king of Øvrebø hillfort on the opposite side of the lake, and king Gause of Gauksland, and Jorunn was the queen of Gauksland, according to Rikard Berge. Arne Vinje, the former gouvernor, tells, that the battle was on the water, so Gause drowned, and that is why he got no mound called after him. Jorunn Joklekåpa is known from a ballad sungen in Telemark, Spind and Agder, where she is an ally of king of Gautland in his war against king Herredag, who’s ally are Thor (the Old Norse god) and Sigurd Svein (Sigurd Fåvnesbane). In another ballad, about count Gunseli, Jorunn Joklekåpa is mentioned too. In the light of that Gunseli is obviously Jormunrek (Ermanaric), she must be Gudrun. According to Molke Moe and Sophus Bugge (see \"Torsvisen i sin norske form\"), the name Joklekåpa is a mix between names of Gjuki and Budli, Brunhild's father in the Völsunga saga.",
                  "source": "commons-desc-page"
                },
